docs/prior_work/legacy/5-riemann_henon/paper_npj/main.tex:49:Since Riemann's 1859 memoir \cite{riemann1859} connected the distribution of primes to the nontrivial zeros of the $\zeta$ function, the Riemann Hypothesis --- that all nontrivial zeros $\rho = 1/2 + i\gamma_n$ lie on the critical line --- has remained central to both mathematics and mathematical physics \cite{titchmarsh1986,sarnak2000}. The Hilbert--P\'olya conjecture offers a physical route to this statement: if the ordinates $\gamma_n$ are the eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator, their reality follows automatically \cite{schumayer2011}. This idea has motivated a long line of candidate operators and quantization schemes, from the Berry--Keating $H = xp$ proposal \cite{berry1999} and its Bender--Brody--M\"uller variant \cite{bender2017}, to the modified-commutator generalizations explored by Bishop et al.\ \cite{bishop2019}, as well as approaches based on noncommutative geometry \cite{connes1999} and Landau levels \cite{sierra2008}. We emphasize at the outset that the present work does \emph{not} claim to identify the Hilbert--P\'olya operator or to prove any statement about the zeros; it explores a specific deterministic dynamical system as a numerical model whose spectrum is compared to the zeros.
