papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:10: \text{ for every }n\in\N\right\}.\] For \(c\in\N\), the decimation \(D_c(x)_n=x_{cn}\) preserves \(X_{a,b}\), so [\[eq:def-X\]](#eq:def-X) is a compact linear multiplicative system. The local rule factors into two first differences after multiplicative coordinates are introduced. That elementary observation is only the starting point: the main result identifies the full finite-projection matroid.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:18: \label{eq:betaF}\end{aligned}\] Thus \(d(F)+\beta(F)=|F|\), and \(\beta(F)\) is the sum of the graph cycle ranks. For precision, the *coordinate-dependence matroid on \(F\)* below is the vector matroid of the evaluation maps \[\epsilon_n:X_{a,b}\longrightarrow\F_q,
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:19: \qquad \epsilon_n(x)=x_n,\qquad n\in F.\] Thus \(A\subseteq F\) is independent when the functionals \(\{\epsilon_n:n\in A\}\) are linearly independent. This is the standard linear-dependence meaning of matroid terminology \[@Whitney1935\].
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:26:2.  For every finite \(F\subset\N\), the image \(\proj_F(X_{a,b})\) is a linear subspace of dimension \(d(F)\). Hence \[\label{eq:finite-count-intro}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:27:     |\proj_F(X_{a,b})|=q^{d(F)}.\] The coordinate-dependence matroid is the direct sum over \(r\) of the graphic matroids of \(G_r(F)\). Equivalently, allowed labels are exactly those whose alternating sum vanishes on every graph cycle.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:94:# Finite projections and graphic matroids
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:111:Distinct root components use disjoint potential variables. Adding the ranks in proves the dimension and count in . We record the complete compatibility description because it also identifies the matroid.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:123:<span id="cor:graphic-matroid" label="cor:graphic-matroid">\[cor:graphic-matroid\]</span> The vector matroid of the restricted evaluation maps \(\{\epsilon_n:n\in F\}\) is \[\label{eq:matroid-direct-sum}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:124: \bigoplus_r M(G_r(F)),\] the direct sum of the graphic matroids of the root-wise incidence graphs. In particular, a subset of coordinates is linearly independent exactly when its root-wise edge sets are forests. Moreover, \[\label{eq:cycle-codimension}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:129:With potentials as columns and edge values as rows, the rows of the matrix in [\[eq:potential-map\]](#eq:potential-map) represent the evaluation maps \(\epsilon_n\) on the finite potential space. After the column-vertex sign change, those rows are the columns of an oriented vertex–edge incidence matrix after transposition. Its column matroid is the graphic matroid \[@Whitney1935\]. This representation remains valid in characteristic two, where the two incidence signs coincide. Roots give block-diagonal matrices and hence the direct sum. A graphic-matroid basis is a maximal spanning forest, and the codimension identity is the Euler formula \(|E|-|V|+c\) summed over roots.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:242:Mora Cuellar, Rojas Aravena, and Yavicoli use prime-valuation coordinates to derive additive and multiplicative density statements, exact finite-coordinate correlations, random models, and symbolic realizations \[@MoraCuellarRojasAravenaYavicoli2026\]. Their work is the closest current source for valuation-coordinate correlations. The present Haar calculation concerns a different object: a finite-field linear plaquette constraint whose arbitrary coordinate dependence is represented by root-wise graphic matroids.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:244:#### Matroid and information-theoretic ingredients.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:246:The linear-dependence language follows Whitney’s matroid framework \[@Whitney1935\], and total correlation is the multivariate entropy deficit introduced by Watanabe \[@Watanabe1960\]. Király, Rosen, and Theran study algebraic matroids and matroids with row–column graph symmetry, motivated in part by matrix completion and rigidity \[@KiralyRosenTheran2013\]. Their framework owns the graph-symmetric matroid neighborhood, but not the arithmetic evaluation matroid of [\[eq:def-X\]](#eq:def-X). Abbe and Spirkl record the general mechanism by which a finite-field representable matroid is realized by an entropy-rank function under uniform linear random variables \[@AbbeSpirkl2019\]. Thus neither incidence-matroid language nor the general linear entropy-rank mechanism is claimed here.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry_round1.md:248:The identity \(y_{i,j}=u_i+v_j\) is likewise an elementary integration of a factorized mixed difference. After these owners are subtracted, the residual P67 statement is the explicit global free-axis homeomorphism for [\[eq:def-X\]](#eq:def-X), the identification of every globally extendable finite projection with the direct sum of the particular graphic matroids \(M(G_r(F))\), and the resulting prefix, rectangle, and Haar forest/cycle formulas.
