papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:19: \qquad \epsilon_n(x)=x_n,\qquad n\in F.\] Thus \(A\subseteq F\) is independent when the functionals \(\{\epsilon_n:n\in A\}\) are linearly independent. This is the standard linear-dependence meaning of matroid terminology \[@Whitney1935\].
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:26:2.  For every finite \(F\subset\N\), the image \(\proj_F(X_{a,b})\) is a linear subspace of dimension \(d(F)\). Hence \[\label{eq:finite-count-intro}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:27:     |\proj_F(X_{a,b})|=q^{d(F)}.\] The coordinate-dependence matroid is the direct sum over \(r\) of the graphic matroids of \(G_r(F)\). Equivalently, allowed labels are exactly those whose alternating sum vanishes on every graph cycle.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:94:# Finite projections and graphic matroids
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:111:Distinct root components use disjoint potential variables. Adding the ranks in proves the dimension and count in . We record the complete compatibility description because it also identifies the matroid.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:123:<span id="cor:graphic-matroid" label="cor:graphic-matroid">\[cor:graphic-matroid\]</span> The vector matroid of the restricted evaluation maps \(\{\epsilon_n:n\in F\}\) is \[\label{eq:matroid-direct-sum}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:124: \bigoplus_r M(G_r(F)),\] the direct sum of the graphic matroids of the root-wise incidence graphs. In particular, a subset of coordinates is linearly independent exactly when its root-wise edge sets are forests. Moreover, \[\label{eq:cycle-codimension}
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:129:With potentials as columns and edge values as rows, the rows of the matrix in [\[eq:potential-map\]](#eq:potential-map) represent the evaluation maps \(\epsilon_n\) on the finite potential space. After the column-vertex sign change, those rows are the columns of an oriented vertex–edge incidence matrix after transposition. Its column matroid is the graphic matroid \[@Whitney1935\]. This representation remains valid in characteristic two, where the two incidence signs coincide. Roots give block-diagonal matrices and hence the direct sum. A graphic-matroid basis is a maximal spanning forest, and the codimension identity is the Euler formula \(|E|-|V|+c\) summed over roots.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:240:Mora Cuellar, Rojas Aravena, and Yavicoli use prime-valuation coordinates to derive additive and multiplicative density statements, exact finite-coordinate correlations, random models, and symbolic realizations \[@MoraCuellarRojasAravenaYavicoli2026\]. Their work is the closest current source for valuation-coordinate correlations. The present Haar calculation concerns a different object: a finite-field linear plaquette constraint whose arbitrary coordinate dependence is represented by root-wise graphic matroids.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:242:#### Standard matroid and information-theoretic ingredients.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:244:The linear-dependence language follows Whitney’s matroid framework \[@Whitney1935\], and total correlation is the multivariate entropy deficit introduced by Watanabe \[@Watanabe1960\]. Incidence matrices representing graphic matroids and the entropy definition are standard ingredients. The identity \(y_{i,j}=u_i+v_j\) is likewise an elementary integration of a factorized mixed difference. The paper-specific result is the explicit arithmetic assembly of these ingredients into the all-finite-shape projection and forest/cycle formulas stated here.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:248:An exact-string and citing-neighborhood search frozen on 25 August 2026 covered the displayed equation, sign and function-notation variants, “multiplicative plaquette,” finite-field linear multiplicative subshifts, the representation \(u_i+v_j\), factorized \((1-u)(1-v)\) constraints, and graph-rank/matroid/correlation formulations. It recovered the context cluster above but no source stating the same equation together with the global free-axis homeomorphism and the all-finite-shape graphic-matroid theorem. This is recorded only as `BOUNDED_NO_EXACT_COLLISION_LOCATED`. Search vocabulary may differ, folklore may exist, and no worldwide novelty conclusion follows.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:270:The arbitrary finite-shape theorem adds the missing local organization. Selected coordinates become edges of root-wise bipartite graphs, allowed labels are vertex-potential coboundaries, and graph cycles are the complete compatibility obstruction. As a result, the coordinate matroid is graphic and normalized Haar dependence is exact: forests give joint independence, while each cycle-rank unit contributes \(\log q\) of total correlation. The pairwise-independent but plaquette-dependent four-corner example is the smallest instance of this rule.
papers/67-multiplicative-plaquette-matroid-complexity/stage2_5/draft_for_claim_registry.md:274:The manuscript makes no priority claim. Its proof package is closed at the internal-draft level, while external release remains contingent on specialist exact-neighbor review in multiplicative symbolic dynamics, algebraic actions, finite-field coding, and matroidal probability.
